{# Executive report. Step 10.2.

   One to two pages, business language, no hex and no packet indices. The
   test for this template is whether a non-technical reader can say what is
   wrong and what to do about it having read only this document -- so every
   finding here leads with its consequence, not its mechanism. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>IPsec security assessment</title></head>
<body>

<h1>IPsec security assessment</h1>
<p class="subtitle">
  Generated {{ generated_at }} · Analysis engine {{ a.engine_version }} ·
  Policy {{ a.policy_version }}
</p>

<div class="score-band">
  <div>
    <div class="score-value rating-{{ a.score.rating }}">{{ a.score.total }}</div>
    <div class="score-label">Security score, out of 100</div>
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="score-value rating-{{ a.score.rating }}" style="font-size: 16pt;">
      {{ a.score.rating | capitalize }}
    </div>
    <div class="score-label">Overall rating</div>
  </div>
  <div>
    <div class="score-value" style="font-size: 16pt;">{{ a.metadata_exposure.score }}</div>
    <div class="score-label">Metadata exposure (higher is worse)</div>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>What this means</h2>
<p>
  {% if a.score.total >= 80 %}
    This deployment is configured to a current standard. The findings below are
    refinements rather than exposures, and none of them requires urgent action.
  {% elif a.score.total >= 60 %}
    This deployment is broadly sound but is running at least one setting that
    is below current guidance. The issues below are worth scheduling, not
    escalating.
  {% elif a.score.total >= 40 %}
    This deployment has weaknesses that a capable attacker could work with.
    The issues below should be scheduled deliberately rather than absorbed into
    routine maintenance.
  {% else %}
    This deployment is running cryptography or protocol settings that are
    considered broken or deprecated today. Traffic protected by it should not be
    assumed confidential against a well-resourced observer, and the actions
    below should be treated as urgent.
  {% endif %}
</p>

{% if top_risks %}
<h2>The three things that matter most</h2>
{% for finding in top_risks %}
<div class="finding">
  <h3><span class="sev sev-{{ finding.severity }}">{{ finding.severity }}</span>
      {{ finding.title }}</h3>
  <p>{{ finding.description | trim }}</p>
  <p><strong>What to do:</strong> {{ finding.remediation | trim }}</p>
</div>
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
<h2>Findings</h2>
<p>
  No policy rule matched this deployment. That is a result rather than an
  absence of checking: {{ rule_count }} rules were evaluated against it.
</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>What a passive observer learns</h2>
<p>{{ a.metadata_exposure.rationale | trim }}</p>
{% if a.metadata_exposure.score >= 50 %}
<div class="callout">
  <p class="small">
    This is worth understanding separately from the score above, because it does
    not improve when the cryptography does. The leak is in the size and timing
    of packets, which encryption does not conceal. If the fact that this tunnel
    carries a particular kind of traffic is itself sensitive, that needs a
    padding strategy, not a stronger cipher.
  </p>
</div>
{% endif %}

<h2>Confidence in these results</h2>
<p>
  This assessment was produced from captured traffic alone -- no access to the
  gateways and no decryption. Some parameters can be read directly from the
  key-exchange negotiation; others can only be estimated from traffic patterns;
  and some cannot be determined at all from a capture.
  {% if unavailable_count %}
    {{ unavailable_count }} parameter{{ 's' if unavailable_count != 1 else '' }}
    in this assessment fall{{ '' if unavailable_count != 1 else 's' }} into that
    last category and {{ 'are' if unavailable_count != 1 else 'is' }} reported as
    undetermined, with the reason, in the technical report.
  {% endif %}
  Nothing has been guessed to fill a gap.
</p>

{% if quality_caveats %}
<div class="callout">
  <p class="small"><strong>Limits of this capture:</strong></p>
  <ul class="small">
    {% for caveat in quality_caveats %}<li>{{ caveat }}</li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
</div>
{% endif %}

</body>
</html>
